WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.020
The only question you need to ask yourself while

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.020
studying the third law is.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.240
What happens when two objects interact with each other?

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.320
What happens when you place an object on the table?

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.600
The object exerts a force on the table.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.280
And the subject exert an upward force on the object.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.760
There are two forces that result from this interaction.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.440
A force on the table and a force on the object.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.000
The magnitudes of the two forces are the same,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.700
but the directions are opposite.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.900
These forces are called as action and reaction forces.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.020
The force exerted by the object on the table was an action force.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.120
And the force exerted by the table on the object is the reaction force.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.160
What we saw here is nothing but a

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.060
display of the Newton's third law of motion.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.440
Every action has an equal and opposite reaction.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.740
That's how simple the third law is.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.880
Every action has an equal and opposite reaction.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.840
It means that when two objects interact there is a force on each object.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.460
The magnitude of force on the first object,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.800
equals the magnitude of force on the second one.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.860
And what about the direction?

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.280
The direction of force on the first object

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.320
is opposite to the direction of force on the second one.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.800
Let's talk about more examples.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.120
While a frog is swimming it pushes the water back.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.800
and the water pushes its body forward.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.420
Now let me ask you a question.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.540
Say a bird is flying upwards,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.920
will the Newton's third law apply here?

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.380
Sometimes the third law is not so obvious.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.380
You'd probably say that the bird is the only object here,

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.700
and there is no second object.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.440
But that's not correct!

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.120
The wings of the bird push the air downwards,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.800
and the air pushes the bird upwards.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.300
Action and reaction forces make it possible for birds to fly.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.520
We take one last example to understand

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.660
something very interesting about the third law.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.280
What happens when a gun is fired?

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.940
You're right it exert the forward force on the bullet.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.780
And the bullet exerts an equal and opposite reaction force on the gun.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.040
And this exists in recoil of the gun.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.820
A perfect example of Newton's third law!

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.060
But hold on, if the force exerted by the gun on the bullet

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.380
is the same as the force exerted by the bullet on the gun,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.680
then why doesn't the gun recoil with the same acceleration as the bullet?

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.420
The gun recoils, yes,

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:47.260
but it doesn't recoil at the same acceleration at the bullet, right?

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.620
Same magnitude of forces but different accelerations.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.900
Why do you think this happens?

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.060
Okay, so if your concept of the second law of motion was clear,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.860
you wouldn't really be surprised with this.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.800
The second law of motion says that

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.280
the force is the product of the object's mass and acceleration.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:15.600
Or the acceleration of the object is equal to the force over its mass.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.160
What does this tell us?

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.880
Yes, if the mass is more then the acceleration will be less.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.420
In this example the mass of the gun is much more than that of the bullet.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.780
Hence, the acceleration of the rifle is lesser than that of the bullet.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.340
If you're in a boat and step out of it onto the land

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.640
there are two forces involved.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.400
You pushing the boat behind and the boat pushing you forward.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.420
And that's why you see the boat go behind when you go forward.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.120
Now let's say a sailor jumps off a huge ship.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.320
The sailor does apply a force on the ship

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.040
and the ship also applies a force on the sailor.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.800
But do you think the ship will accelerate as much as the sailor?

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.160
Not really and I think you know why.

